i## title: My Gopher application framework	/	tilde.club	70
i## date: "2026-06-10"	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i## Introduction	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iI've always really liked the Gopher protocol for its	/	tilde.club	70
isimplicity, lightness, and accessibility. A while back, I	/	tilde.club	70
iwanted to have my own solution for creating custom Gopher	/	tilde.club	70
iapplications. That's why I decided to write a framework in	/	tilde.club	70
iGo that allows you to implement an RFC 1436 compliant Gopher	/	tilde.club	70
iserver, as well as add evaluation extensions for the	/	tilde.club	70
igophermap format and dynamic and virtual routes.	/	tilde.club	70
hRFC 1436	URL:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1436.html	www.rfc-editor.org	443
i	/	tilde.club	70
iThe name of the project is fleur, pronounced \\flœʁ\\, which	/	tilde.club	70
imeans flower.	/	tilde.club	70
hfleur	URL:https://github.com/theobori/fleur	github.com	443
i	/	tilde.club	70
i## My goal	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iMy goal is to provide an API for implementing Gopher	/	tilde.club	70
iapplications. I want it to be easy to use while remaining	/	tilde.club	70
ilow-level enough to allow for a high degree of flexibility.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i## How it works	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iThere are a few core components to introduce in order to	/	tilde.club	70
ifully understand how the framework works.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Route	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iA route is a pair consisting of a regular expression and a	/	tilde.club	70
ifunction used by a router.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Router	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iThis is an interface that manages routes. If one of the	/	tilde.club	70
iregexes matches the request path, the associated function is	/	tilde.club	70
icalled. For example, a client requesting a path matching the	/	tilde.club	70
iregex ^/dice$ would receive a gophermap page containing the	/	tilde.club	70
iresult of a dice roll.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Server	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iA server is essential since it is responsible for receiving	/	tilde.club	70
iGopher requests from clients and responding to them. Before	/	tilde.club	70
iserving a file, it will ask its router if there is a routing	/	tilde.club	70
irule for the path requested by the client. If there is one,	/	tilde.club	70
ithe associated function will be called first to respond to	/	tilde.club	70
ithe client.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iNote that before serving a directory, the server will check	/	tilde.club	70
iif there is a gophermap file at its root; if so, it will be	/	tilde.club	70
iserved instead of the directory.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iAlso, only files named gophermap and those with the	/	tilde.club	70
i.gophermap extension will be evaluated.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Extension	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iAn extension is a pair consisting of a regex and a function;	/	tilde.club	70
iit is similar to the routes in the router component, except	/	tilde.club	70
ithat here, an extension is specific to the gophermap format.	/	tilde.club	70
iCreating an extension allows you, for example, to add,	/	tilde.club	70
imodify, or delete gophermap item types. For instance, all	/	tilde.club	70
istandard RFC 1436 item types are implemented using	/	tilde.club	70
iextensions in the Fleur CLI.	/	tilde.club	70
hRFC 1436	URL:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1436.html	www.rfc-editor.org	443
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Extension Manager	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iThis is an interface for managing extensions; it is used by	/	tilde.club	70
ithe evaluator to extend the gophermap.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Evaluator	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iAn evaluator evaluates gophermap files when the server is	/	tilde.club	70
iready to serve them. This evaluation works line by line and	/	tilde.club	70
iallows the gophermap format to be extended by adding	/	tilde.club	70
iextensions.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Example of a simple Gopher Application	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iBelow is a very simple example of a Gopher application	/	tilde.club	70
idesigned to serve files.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
ipackage main	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iimport (	/	tilde.club	70
i    "flag"	/	tilde.club	70
i    "log"	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i    "github.com/theobori/fleur/gopher"	/	tilde.club	70
i    "github.com/theobori/fleur/gophermap/evaluator"	/	tilde.club	70
i    "github.com/theobori/fleur/server"	/	tilde.club	70
i)	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
ifunc main() {	/	tilde.club	70
i    var (	/	tilde.club	70
i        err           error	/	tilde.club	70
i        directoryPath string	/	tilde.club	70
i        domain        string	/	tilde.club	70
i        port          int	/	tilde.club	70
i    )	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i    flag.StringVar(	/	tilde.club	70
i        &directoryPath,	/	tilde.club	70
i        "directory",	/	tilde.club	70
i        "./fleur",	/	tilde.club	70
i        "Root directory of the Gopher server",	/	tilde.club	70
i    )	/	tilde.club	70
i    flag.StringVar(	/	tilde.club	70
i        &domain,	/	tilde.club	70
i        "domain",	/	tilde.club	70
i        "localhost",	/	tilde.club	70
i        "Gopher domain",	/	tilde.club	70
i    )	/	tilde.club	70
i    flag.IntVar(	/	tilde.club	70
i        &port,	/	tilde.club	70
i        "port",	/	tilde.club	70
i        gopher.DefaultPort,	/	tilde.club	70
i        "Gopher port",	/	tilde.club	70
i    )	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i    flag.Parse()	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i    serverOptions, err := server.NewOptions(	/	tilde.club	70
i        port,	/	tilde.club	70
i        directoryPath,	/	tilde.club	70
i        domain,	/	tilde.club	70
i        true,	/	tilde.club	70
i    )	/	tilde.club	70
i    if err != nil {	/	tilde.club	70
i        log.Fatalln(err)	/	tilde.club	70
i    }	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i    evaluatorOptions := evaluator.Options{	/	tilde.club	70
i        Port:                 serverOptions.Port,	/	tilde.club	70
i        DirectoryPath:        serverOptions.DirectoryPath,	/	tilde.club	70
i        Domain:               serverOptions.Domain,	/	tilde.club	70
i        EnableAutoInlineText: true,	/	tilde.club	70
i    }	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i    em := evaluator.RFC1436ItemsExtensionManager()	/	tilde.club	70
i    evaluator := evaluator.NewEvaluator(&evaluatorOptions, em)	/	tilde.club	70
i    server := server.NewServer(serverOptions, evaluator)	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i    err = server.Serve()	/	tilde.club	70
i    if err != nil {	/	tilde.club	70
i        log.Fatalln(err)	/	tilde.club	70
i    }	/	tilde.club	70
i}	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iOnce compiled, the application can be launched using the	/	tilde.club	70
icommand line below.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i./simple -port 7070 -directory "./"	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i## CLI	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iThe fleur CLI is a Gopher application that serves files	/	tilde.club	70
iimplemented with the framework. It optionally supports	/	tilde.club	70
ipersonal Gopherspaces on UNIX systems, with the virtual path	/	tilde.club	70
i/~username/ being converted to /home/username/public_gopher.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iIt also implements an extension that lists files in the	/	tilde.club	70
icurrent directory. To use it, type * on a line. This feature	/	tilde.club	70
iis inspired by gophernicus.	/	tilde.club	70
hgophernicus	URL:https://github.com/gophernicus/gophernicus	github.com	443
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### Help Message	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iBelow is the CLI help message.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iUsage of ./fleur:	/	tilde.club	70
i  -directory string	/	tilde.club	70
i        It specifies an input directory path that will be the	/	tilde.club	70
iroot of the Gopher server (default "./fleur")	/	tilde.club	70
i  -domain string	/	tilde.club	70
i        Gopher domain (default "localhost")	/	tilde.club	70
i  -enable-auto-inline-text	/	tilde.club	70
i        Relax non compliant text error and convert to gophermap	/	tilde.club	70
iinline text	/	tilde.club	70
i  -enable-personal-gopherspaces	/	tilde.club	70
i        Enable personal Gopherspaces, it allows each user of	/	tilde.club	70
ithe system to serve its own files	/	tilde.club	70
i  -port int	/	tilde.club	70
i        Gopher port (default 70)	/	tilde.club	70
i  -verbose	/	tilde.club	70
i        Enable verbose logs	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i### NixOS module	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iI also provide a NixOS module for managing the fleur CLI	/	tilde.club	70
iusing a systemd service unit. Inside your flake.nix, you can	/	tilde.club	70
istart by adding the following lines.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i{	/	tilde.club	70
i  inputs = {	/	tilde.club	70
i    fleur.url = "github:theobori/fleur";	/	tilde.club	70
i  };	/	tilde.club	70
i}	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iYou can then include the module and its default overlay in	/	tilde.club	70
ithe system configuration as shown below.	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i{ inputs, ... }: {	/	tilde.club	70
i  imports = with inputs; [ fleur.nixosModules.default ];	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i  services.fleur = {	/	tilde.club	70
i    enable = true;	/	tilde.club	70
i    directory = "${./my-directory}";	/	tilde.club	70
i    port = 7070;	/	tilde.club	70
i    verbose = true;	/	tilde.club	70
i    autoInlineText = true;	/	tilde.club	70
i    personalGopherspaces = true;	/	tilde.club	70
i    # Other options	/	tilde.club	70
i  };	/	tilde.club	70
i}	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
i## Conclusion	/	tilde.club	70
i	/	tilde.club	70
iIt was a fun project to write and test, and I plan to use it	/	tilde.club	70
isoon to serve my own gophermap files. Also, thanks to	/	tilde.club	70
iWireshark, it was a huge help.	/	tilde.club	70
